<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Martinus Theunis Steyn</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1857">1857</a>–<a href="/years/1916">1916</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as M. T. Steyn</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Martinus Theunis Steyn was a lawyer and the last president of the Orange Free State, serving from 1896 to 1902. He allied the republic with the South African Republic and led it into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a> in 1899, staying in the field with the commandos after the British occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloemfontein">Bloemfontein</a>. He later took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-convention">National Convention</a> that prepared the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/martinus-theunis-steyn.jpg" alt="Martinus Theunis Steyn" width="246" height="300" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marthinus_Steyn,_Orange_Free_State_president.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Steyn was born on 2 October 1857 on the farm Rietfontein near Winburg, where his mother Cecilia was staying with her sister Gertruida and her husband Theunis Wessels while his father Marthinus was away in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>; he was named after both men. During the Free State–Basotho War of 1858 the family was driven from its farm and lost almost everything, and he grew up on the farm Zuurfontein on the Modder River near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloemfontein">Bloemfontein</a>.</p><p>After Grey College and four years of farming, he was persuaded by Judge James Buchanan of the High Court to study law. He sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in May 1877, studied at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">Gymnasium</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deventer">Deventer</a>, then entered the Inner Temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and was called to the English bar in November 1882. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloemfontein">Bloemfontein</a> he practised as a barrister, became state attorney in 1889 and first puisne judge of the high court in 1893.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>When President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-william-reitz">Francis William Reitz</a> resigned in 1895, Steyn stood as the candidate of the pan-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> party and won the election of February 1896 decisively. At the outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a> in 1899 he tied his state to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>, and once the British occupied the Orange Free State he ran his government from the field, helping to coordinate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare that made up most of the war from 1900.</p><p>Counted among the most irreconcilable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> leaders, he took part in the preliminary peace talks at Klerksdorp in April 1902, but illness kept him from signing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-vereeniging">Treaty of Vereeniging</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a> on 31 May 1902. The treaty ended the independence of the Orange Free State, and with it his term as president.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Suffering from myasthenia gravis, he stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> from July 1902 to the autumn of 1904, then took the oath of allegiance to the British crown and returned to politics. He was vice-president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-convention">National Convention</a> of 1908–1909, and in 1914 left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-african-party">South African Party</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/j-b-m-hertzog">James Barry Munnik Hertzog</a> and Christiaan Rudolf de Wet to found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a>. He died of a heart attack while addressing a meeting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloemfontein">Bloemfontein</a> in November 1916.</p><p>He initiated the C&amp;N Oranje Meisieskool, a girls' school opened in 1907, and with Emily Hobhouse inspired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-womens-monument">National Women's Monument</a>, unveiled on 16 December 1913. The University of the Free State, established in 1904, answered the hope for a university at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloemfontein">Bloemfontein</a> that he voiced at his inauguration in 1896.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Martinus Theunis Steyn” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His successors, Francis William Reitz and from 1896 <b>Martinus Theunis Steyn</b>, moved closer to the South African Republic, and in 1897 the two states concluded a defensive alliance that bound the Free State to its neighbour’s fortunes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/orange-free-state">Orange Free State · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Winburg, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloemfontein">Bloemfontein</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-28.53,27.02&amp;year=1895" aria-label="Winburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5257 3208 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Martinus Theunis Steyn"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5707 3478h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5707 3478h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-28.53,27.02&amp;year=1895">What was here in 1895? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
